  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday night through Friday)\nIssued at 1018 PM CDT Fri Aug 7 2026\n\nA broad area of high pressure across the Southern CONUS will \ngradually retrograde to the west by late the remainder of next\nweek. The combination of the deep, tropical moisture in place \n(PWATs over 2\") and subtle shortwaves rounding the eastern edge of\nthe ridge mid to late week will create medium chance (40-60%) for\nafternoon/evening showers and thunderstorms each afternoon/evening.\nAs has been the case for much of the month of August, diurnal \nheating will warm temperatures into the upper 80s to lower 90s, \nwith a moderately unstable environment developing each afternoon. \nThus, we will be primed for pulse convection each day (some of \nwhich could be strong to marginally severe) capable of locally \nheavy downpours and gusty winds, \n\nIn addition, highs peaking in the upper 80s to lower 90s and \ndewpoints in the 70s will create peak heat index values between 98 to\n105 degrees each afternoon. NWS Heat Risk highlights a moderate \n(Level 2/4) threat level in most locations Tuesday through Friday, \nwith a few of the metro areas (MSL, DCU, HSV) in the Major (Level \n3/4) category.",
